---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import Indeterminate from "../../component-examples/progress/Indeterminate.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="progress-native">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> comes with the right role, a value screen readers
      announce and an indeterminate state when there is no <code>value</code>.
      But every engine draws it differently, and the indeterminate animation is
      whatever the browser ships.
    </p>
    <p>
      So most libraries swap it for a <code>&lt;div role="progressbar"&gt;</code
      >{" "}
      and keep the ARIA attributes in sync by hand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>appearance: none</code> drops the native look, a wrapper draws the track
        and clips the bar to its radius
      </li>
      <li>
        An <code>outline</code> in the field border color gives the track 3:1. It
        sits outside the box, so <code>overflow: hidden</code> doesn't clip it
      </li>
      <li>
        The value is a vendor pseudo-element: <code
          >::-webkit-progress-value</code
        > in Chromium and Safari, <code>::-moz-progress-bar</code> in Firefox
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:indeterminate</code> matches a <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> without
        a <code>value</code>, and <code>:has()</code> lets the wrapper react to it
      </li>
      <li>
        The wrapper's <code>::after</code> slides across by animating <code
          >inset-inline-start</code
        > and <code>inset-inline-end</code>, so it runs the other way in
        right-to-left
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.progress > progress {
  appearance: none;
  background: none;
  border: 0;
}

.progress > progress[value]::-webkit-progress-value {
  background-color: var(--ui-primary);
}

.progress > progress::-moz-progress-bar {
  background-color: var(--ui-primary);
}

.progress:has(> progress:indeterminate)::after {
  animation: indeterminate 2s linear infinite;
  background-color: var(--ui-primary);
  content: "";
  inset-block: 0;
  position: absolute;
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <p>A native <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> without a <code>value</code>.</p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <Indeterminate />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Each vendor pseudo-element has its own rule, so an engine drops only the
        one it doesn't know instead of the whole selector list
      </li>
      <li>
        Without <code>:has()</code> the indeterminate rules drop out and the wrapper
        never draws its sliding bar
      </li>
      <li>
        Under reduced motion the bar pulses in place instead of sliding.
        Browsers without container style queries skip the pulse
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
